## Why the validator was vendored

Upstream conditions use of the validator on **"From a repository checkout"** in both
the six rules and taste gate (`SKILL.md:289` and `:482`). **Marketplace installations
therefore lack the validator.** The skill's `scripts/` contains only
`drawio_extract.py`, `mermaid_extract.py`, and `self_check.py`; the validator lives
only in the repository-root `scripts/`.

Copying six rules alone would leave **checklist recitation without a validator**.
We imported it under MIT and **included its test file** so deterioration would remain detectable.

## Example-asset integrity

The four example assets are derivatives with two exact paired-sentinel comment lines:
`<!-- verify-connectors:legend:BEGIN -->` and `<!-- verify-connectors:legend:END -->`.
Machine-readable `# sentinel-stripped-sha256 <sha256> <path>` records in
`UPSTREAM.sha256` hash the bytes after removing only those two **complete lines**.
`tests/run_tests.py` requires exactly one case-sensitive BEGIN and END line in each
asset, then compares the hash preserving every other byte with that record.

For `example-architecture.html`, `example-dp-integration.html`, and
`example-sequence-oauth.html`, the stripped hashes equal the original upstream hashes.
For `example-swimlane.html`, the stripped hash covers the current geometry-modified
derivative. Its original upstream hash and the reason for the orthogonal L-path
change enforcing the diagonal prohibition remain separately recorded in the manifest.

`UPSTREAM.sha256` is not a normalized-hash list: ordinary entries represent only
**imports whose raw bytes match upstream**. The four derivative examples are not
ordinary entries. The sole current raw-byte entry is `assets/upstream/verify-geometry.py.txt`.
`tests/run_tests.py` separately pins connector counts in the four derivatives.

