## up:archify (imported 2026-08-30)

- Source: https://github.com/tt-a1i/archify — MIT.
- Dual copyright: 2026 tt-a1i (Archify) and 2025 Cocoon AI, original author of
  "architecture-diagram-generator". Full text: `LICENSE.archify`.
- Clone commit: `0853a805003514776bef3593ecca091409828902` (2026-08-29).
  Hashes are recorded in `UPSTREAM.sha256`.
- **Four areas were imported: orthogonality checks; overlap/corridor/crossing checks;
  five-type placement grammar (documentation, unenforced); and visual-review contracts
  (documentation, unenforced).** Visual review moved to the delivery section in
  `references/taste-gate.md`. The upstream `visual-check` runtime was not imported:
  this implementation uses Python and adds no Node dependency. Status is self-reported.
  Placement grammar moved to `references/type-placement.md` and **has no validator**;
  upstream's `validate`/`deliver` pipeline was not imported. This is disclosed in
  `SKILL.md` Limitations and at the bottom of that reference. Upstream has a 16,200-line
  Node runtime versus this 2,139-line lightweight skill, so wholesale adoption would
  replace the skill rather than absorb selected behavior. Renderers, viewer runtime,
  delivery pipeline, brand marks, and i18n were not imported. Upstream i18n specifies
  `SUPPORTED_LOCALES = ['en','zh-CN']` without Korean, which the local font contract covers.
- Rewritten in Python. Upstream identifies arrows with custom classes such as
  `a-default`; local SVG lacks them, so `marker-end` determines membership. Only valid,
  non-nested pairs of the case-sensitive markers
  `<!-- verify-connectors:legend:BEGIN -->` and `<!-- verify-connectors:legend:END -->`
  exclude legend sample lines. Generic `Legend` comments have no machine meaning;
  orphan, reversed, or nested markers fail closed with `input/legend-markup`.
- **The label-width formula (H-3, 2026-08-31) is also a rewrite.** `textUnits()` preserves
  the FULLWIDTH ranges and variation-selector rules, but **the width formula deliberately
  diverges**. Enforcing upstream's approximate mask width of 6.5px × ASCII units + 13px
  produced false positives for local 7–8px labels: eight of 11 masks in
  `example-architecture.html`. The implementation instead uses a font-size-scaled
  model: ASCII 0.6em, full-width 1em, 4px slack. The upstream empirical formula remains
  only in documentation and diagnostic `evidence`. Full-width weighting uses 1.0em
  from local `output-spec.md`, not upstream's converted 1.2em.
- **The three source files above have recorded sha256 values but are not entries in
  `UPSTREAM.sha256`.** That manifest contracts byte-identical imports, currently only
  `assets/upstream/verify-geometry.py.txt`. Those three hashes record *provenance* for Python rewrites;
  the upstream files themselves are absent and cannot be bound as local byte contracts.
- **The diagnostic schema from `shared/diagnostics.mjs` is also imported** (2026-08-31).
  Envelope fields for `verify-connectors.py --json` follow upstream
  `normalizedDiagnostic` / `rendererFailure`: `schemaVersion`, `ok`, `source`, and
  `diagnostics[]{code,severity,message,subject,evidence,supportedFixes}`.
  **There are two intentional divergences.**
  (a) Upstream `supportedFixes` is a string array; the local schema uses an **object array**
  so machines can read the five repair stages' order and `requiresEvidence` for deletion.
  **Ordered repair stages are themselves a local addition, absent upstream.** Feeding
  the local envelope directly to upstream tools will therefore break them.
  (b) Upstream enables JSON with `ARCHIFY_DIAGNOSTIC_FORMAT=json` for renderer subprocesses.
  The local tool is a human-invoked CLI and uses `--json` instead.

## Node runtime migration

Executable geometry, connector, width, label, scale, effective-size, PNG, and
viewer-URL checks are TypeScript sources emitted as JavaScript for Node >=20.
The 168-line upstream validator and 142-line adapted test remain non-executable
provenance text under `assets/upstream/`. Their original bytes and line counts
remain independently measured; the JavaScript validator is tested directly with
all original fixtures and an injected empty-verdict mutation. No byte-identical
claim applies to the TypeScript ports. Existing upstream MIT notices remain in
`LICENSE.upstream` and `LICENSE.archify`.
